 Fever and constitutional symptoms could suggest sepsis, but labs show leukocytosis with blasts rather than leukocytosis without blasts", "Myeloproliferative neoplasm: Leukocytosis might suggest this, but presence of blasts (35%) points more strongly toward acute leukemia", "Metastatic visceral mass syndrome: Weight loss and systemic symptoms could align, but laboratory profile shows hematologic (blasts) rather than visceral organ failure", "Pancytopenia (original root phrase): Inaccurate due to leukocytosis, not leukopenia"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss)", "Severe leukocytosis with blasts (35% immature cells)", "Bimodal hematologic disturbance (anemia + thrombocytopenia in context of leukocytosis)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.57791421229492, "posterior": 0.57791421229492, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.85, "interrupt_relevance": 0.95, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.028870330104763788, "posterior": 0.028870330104763788, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "lymphoblastic lymphoma"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.021, "danger": 0.65, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.01, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.004, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2", "B3", "B4", "B5"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 35% blast count in peripheral blood argue against MDS-EB (10-19% blasts typical) in favor of AML (higher blast thresholds)?", "summary": "The blast percentage exceeds typical MDS-EB thresholds (10-19%), shifting probability toward de novo AML."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "No findings directly contradict de novo AML, as the clinical and laboratory profile aligns with its pathophysiology."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 35% blasts in peripheral blood and subacute onset (days) with pancytopenia (Hgb 10 g/dL, Hct 31%, platelets 109,000/mm³) strongly support de novo AML over MDS-EB and CML-BC?", "summary": "35% blasts exceed MDS-EB thresholds (10-19%) and lack chronic CML features (splenomegaly, basophilia) argue against CML in blast crisis. This supports de novo AML as the most likely explanation for the blastemia and pancytopenia."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 35% blasts in peripheral blood (exceeding MDS-EB thresholds of 10-19%) rule out myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts as a dangerous alternative?", "summary": "35% blasts exceed WHO criteria for MDS-EB (10-19%) but do not definitively rule out secondary AML (which includes MDS-EB1/2). The high blast count shifts probability toward de novo AML rather than MDS-EB as the primary explanation."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset (several days) with no chronic CML phase features (e.g., splenomegaly, basophilia) support de novo AML over CML in blast crisis?", "summary": "Absence of chronic CML features (splenomegaly, basophilia) and subacute presentation weakens CML in blast crisis as a potential explanation. This supports de novo AML as a more consistent diagnosis with the acute presentation."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "No findings directly contradict de novo AML. The clinical and laboratory profile (35% blasts, pancytopenia, constitutional symptoms) aligns with its pathophysiology, though further testing is needed to confirm."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.697], ["B1.1", 0.384], ["B2", 0.205]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.492], ["B1.1", 0.371], ["B2", 0.088]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.552], ["B1.1", 0.491], ["B2", 0.052]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability detected. Patient is hemodynamically stable (BP 122/90 mmHg, HR 84/min, SpO2 99% on room air). Airway and respiratory function are intact. No focal neurological deficits, uncontrolled hemorrhage, or end-organ damage from shock. Time-critical conditions (STEMI, stroke, septic shock) are not indicated by current findings.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Root posterior (0.552) remains <0.75 and high-danger branch B1.1 (AML) still has posterior 0.491. Bone marrow biopsy (requestable_discriminator) is untried but prerequisites (peripheral smear review) are not yet completed. No actionable parent-level management exists without definitive lineage confirmation."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.92, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4910592226915738, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Acute myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'leukemia', 'Myeloid leukemia', 'multiple myeloma', 'Acute myeloid leukemia', 'bone pain'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.697}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "posterior": 0.578, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "confirm"}], "considered_alternatives": []}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=primekg]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, elevated serum creatinine, fatigue\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase): bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → pain → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
